_ivp("Debug: duplicate tickets are not merging",
 "A customer engineer wrote <code>merge_tickets</code> and left. Support says duplicates are still showing and the wrong ticket id is kept. <b>Read the docstring, run the checks, fix the function.</b> You did not write this code; do not rewrite it from scratch.",
 "merge_tickets",
 "def merge_tickets(tickets):\n    \"\"\"tickets: list of dicts with 'id', 'customer', 'subject', 'created' ('YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM').\n    Tickets from the same customer with the same subject are duplicates;\n    compare subjects ignoring case and surrounding spaces.\n    Return one dict per group: {'id': id of the EARLIEST ticket, 'customer', 'subject' (cleaned,\n    lower-case), 'count': group size}. Sort by the earliest created time, then id.\"\"\"\n    groups = {}\n    for t in tickets:\n        key = (t['customer'], t['subject'].lower())\n        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(t)\n    out = []\n    for (customer, subject), items in groups.items():\n        first = items[0]\n        out.append({'id': first['id'], 'customer': customer, 'subject': subject, 'count': len(items), 'at': first['created']})\n    out.sort(key=lambda r: r['id'])\n    for r in out:\n        del r['at']\n    return out\n",
 "def merge_tickets(tickets):\n    groups = {}\n    for t in tickets:\n        key = (t['customer'], t['subject'].strip().lower())\n        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(t)\n    out = []\n    for (customer, subject), items in groups.items():\n        first = min(items, key=lambda t: (t['created'], t['id']))\n        out.append({'id': first['id'], 'customer': customer, 'subject': subject, 'count': len(items), 'at': first['created']})\n    out.sort(key=lambda r: (r['at'], r['id']))\n    for r in out:\n        del r['at']\n    return out\n",
 [("no duplicates", "merge_tickets(TK[:2])"), ("case and spaces are ignored", "merge_tickets([TK[0], TK[2]])"), ("keeps the earliest ticket, not the first in the list", "merge_tickets([TK[3], TK[0]])"), ("sorted by earliest time", "merge_tickets(TK)"), ("different customers never merge", "merge_tickets([TK[0], TK[4]])"), ("empty", "merge_tickets([])")],
 "There are three separate faults: how subjects are compared, which ticket is treated as first, and what the final sort uses.",
 prelude="TK = [{'id': 5, 'customer': 'acme', 'subject': 'Late delivery', 'created': '2026-03-02 10:00'}, {'id': 2, 'customer': 'bolt', 'subject': 'Invoice wrong', 'created': '2026-03-03 09:00'}, {'id': 7, 'customer': 'acme', 'subject': ' late delivery ', 'created': '2026-03-02 11:30'}, {'id': 9, 'customer': 'acme', 'subject': 'LATE DELIVERY', 'created': '2026-03-01 08:00'}, {'id': 1, 'customer': 'cora', 'subject': 'Late delivery', 'created': '2026-03-05 12:00'}]\n")

_ivs("SQL: each customer's most recent order",
 "The account manager asks: <i>\"For every customer, when did they last order and how much was it? I need the ones who never ordered in the list too.\"</i> Return <code>name</code>, the date of their most recent order and the amount of that order. Customers with no orders appear once with empty (NULL) date and amount.",
 "SELECT c.name, o.order_date, o.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id AND o.order_date = (SELECT MAX(order_date) FROM orders WHERE customer_id = c.id)",
 "Keep every customer with a LEFT JOIN, and match only the order whose date is that customer's latest.")

