_ivp("Debug: overdue tickets report misses open tickets",
 "<code>sla_breaches</code> lists tickets that broke their response-time promise. The support lead says it crashes on tickets that are still open, and the worst tickets appear last. <b>Read the docstring, run the checks, fix the function.</b>",
 "sla_breaches",
 "from datetime import datetime\n\ndef sla_breaches(tickets, now, sla_hours):\n    \"\"\"tickets: list of dicts with 'id', 'priority', 'opened', 'closed'.\n    Times are text 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM'; 'closed' is None while the ticket is still open.\n    sla_hours maps a priority to the allowed hours; a priority not in it uses 48.\n    A ticket is breached when (closed, or now if still open) minus opened is MORE than\n    the allowed hours. Exactly on the limit is fine.\n    Return the ids of breached tickets, most overdue first; ties by id.\"\"\"\n    fmt = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M'\n    late = []\n    for t in tickets:\n        end = datetime.strptime(t['closed'], fmt)\n        hours = (end - datetime.strptime(t['opened'], fmt)).total_seconds() / 3600\n        allowed = sla_hours[t['priority']]\n        if hours >= allowed:\n            late.append((hours - allowed, t['id']))\n    late.sort()\n    return [i for _, i in late]\n",
 "from datetime import datetime\n\ndef sla_breaches(tickets, now, sla_hours):\n    fmt = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M'\n    late = []\n    for t in tickets:\n        end = datetime.strptime(t['closed'] or now, fmt)\n        hours = (end - datetime.strptime(t['opened'], fmt)).total_seconds() / 3600\n        allowed = sla_hours.get(t['priority'], 48)\n        if hours > allowed:\n            late.append((-(hours - allowed), t['id']))\n    late.sort()\n    return [i for _, i in late]\n",
 [("closed tickets", "sla_breaches(SL[:2], NOW, SLA)"), ("open tickets use now", "sla_breaches([SL[2]], NOW, SLA)"), ("exactly on the limit is fine", "sla_breaches([SL[3]], NOW, SLA)"), ("unknown priority uses 48 hours", "sla_breaches([SL[4]], NOW, SLA)"), ("most overdue first", "sla_breaches(SL, NOW, SLA)"), ("ties by id", "sla_breaches([SL[6], SL[5]], NOW, SLA)"), ("nothing late", "sla_breaches([SL[3]], NOW, SLA)"), ("empty", "sla_breaches([], NOW, SLA)")],
 "Four faults: open tickets, the exact-limit rule, priorities missing from the table, and the direction of the sort.",
 prelude="NOW = '2026-03-10 12:00'\nSLA = {'high': 4, 'normal': 24}\nSL = [{'id': 1, 'priority': 'high', 'opened': '2026-03-09 08:00', 'closed': '2026-03-09 15:00'}, {'id': 2, 'priority': 'normal', 'opened': '2026-03-08 08:00', 'closed': '2026-03-08 20:00'}, {'id': 3, 'priority': 'high', 'opened': '2026-03-10 02:00', 'closed': None}, {'id': 4, 'priority': 'high', 'opened': '2026-03-09 08:00', 'closed': '2026-03-09 12:00'}, {'id': 5, 'priority': 'vip', 'opened': '2026-03-07 10:00', 'closed': None}, {'id': 6, 'priority': 'normal', 'opened': '2026-03-08 10:00', 'closed': '2026-03-09 12:00'}, {'id': 7, 'priority': 'normal', 'opened': '2026-03-07 10:00', 'closed': '2026-03-08 12:00'}]\n")

_ivs("SQL: which cities bring in the money",
 "The sales director asks: <i>\"What share of our revenue comes from each city?\"</i> Return each <code>city</code> that has orders, its total order amount, and that total as a percentage of all order revenue, rounded to 1 decimal place.",
 "SELECT c.city, SUM(o.amount), ROUND(100.0 * SUM(o.amount) / (SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders), 1) FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id GROUP BY c.city",
 "Group the joined rows by city; divide each city's total by the total of the whole orders table, which a subquery can give you.")

